filed `human` still moves the wheel. Harness-injected prompts are classified as harness turns, never as a human take: a prompt whose raw start (no trim) is one of `viola-agent-claude`'s four compiled `HARNESS_PREFIXES` — `<agent-message from=`, `<task-notification>`, `<\cross-session-message` or `<cross-session-message` (another Claude session's message; the unescaped form is the one measured from the CLI on 2.1.287, the escaped form is relayed and kept compiled, see Tag escaping). The side effect was accepted with the widening (the founder, live, 2026-09-29 15:21:44): a human who types the cross-session tag at a prompt's start is filed `harness`. As measured on 2.1.287, and not a new ratification, a human who types `<task-notification>` at a prompt's start is filed `harness` too. The wheel starts with `driver` when `viola run` starts, so automation is accepted until the first human editing key. A `send` while a turn is running (after `prompt-submitted`, before `turn-ended`) is not queued. It is refused `not-delivered` with detail `turn-running`, and the driver calls `wait` first. A `send` that arrives while another `send` is in flight (accepted, its outcome not yet recorded) is refused the same way, never queued, and nothing is typed for it. A `prompt-submitted` of any origin marks a turn running, and `turn-ended`, `session-start` or `session-end` ends it. A turn can end with no `turn-ended`: Claude Code's Stop hook does not fire on a user interrupt, and which hook, if any, fires for a turn that ends on an API error is a ledger row (if one does, `viola-agent-claude` registers it and maps it to `turn-ended` with a `null` `last_assistant_message`). A human interrupt is human input, so it moves the wheel to the human, and the `viola release` that returns the wheel clears the running-turn state. As built (as measured at chunk 2026-10-04-running-turn-refusal), the running-turn state lives in memory in `WheelSlot` beside the holder and cause, and records no event, snapshot field, span or obs line, so a restarted wrapper starts with no turn. The wrapper's one hook entry (`run::send::append_hook_event`) marks it before the hook's line is appended, keyed on the line's kind alone: a `prompt-submitted` of any origin (the claimed driver prompt included) marks it running, `turn-ended`, `session-start` or `session-end` ends it, and `activity` leaves it. So a driver that read `turn-ended` through `wait` is never refused by that turn. The turn is marked ahead of a human prompt's wheel move, so a `release` landing between the two still clears it. `send` reads the wheel and then the running turn twice, in that order both times: at the send's arrival, and again once the readiness gate's wait (up to `GATE_MAX_WAIT`, 8.5 s) has returned ready. A human key or a turn that started during the wait is refused `human-typing` or `not-delivered` / `turn-running`, with no `send-issued` and nothing typed (the overseer's technical answer of 2026-10-07T12:10Z at chunk 2026-10-07-send-waits-out-the-paste-hint, listed for the founder as a fact; it retires the residual recorded on 2026-10-04, under which a turn starting during the wait was not refused). The wait is not interrupted when the wheel moves: `Gate` knows nothing of the wheel, so the driver hears the refusal when the wait ends. Only a `release` that returns a human-held wheel clears the turn, in the same lock hold as the move. A `release` on a wheel the driver already holds, `release --budget` and a refused `release` (`release-from-driver`, invalid params) leave it, as do `pause` and human keys, so a turn left running under a driver-held wheel is recovered without a keystroke by `viola pause` then `viola release`. An unsent human prompt moves the wheel before its line is appended, so the `send` after it reads `human-typing`, never `turn-running`, and the `release` that returns the wheel also clears that turn. `release` never empties the in-flight `send` slot, which belongs to a live send. A SessionEnd the hook appends directly (the channel did not take it) leaves the turn marked — a recorded residual; its child is ending, so no driver `send` depends on it. A driver parked in `wait` across such a turn recovers through `timeout_ms`. CLI `viola pause` moves the wheel to the human without a keystroke, after which automated `send` is refused `human-typing` with detail `manual-pause`. It is the CLI twin of the reserved v1.x GUI brake route. The wheel returns only through CLI `viola release`; a `release` on a wheel the driver already holds changes nothing and appends nothing. The wheel lives in `src/run/wheel.rs` (`WheelSlot`: holder, cause and the running turn under one lock). The in-memory move is synchronous; each change's `wheel` event (`{holder, cause}`, `source:"wrapper"`, log-only, never through the wait feed), its snapshot update, its `run.wheel_transition` span and the pending-dialog hand-back run on the wheel's own worker thread, and `viola run` flushes that worker (bounded 2 s) at exit before the terminal leaves raw mode. Every snapshot write goes through the one in-process holder `src/run/snapshot.rs` (`Snapshots`). While the wheel is `human`, the wrapper answers `hook.dialog` with `null` at once, so the dialog renders for the human without waiting out the dialog deadline, and `answer` is refused `human-typing`. If the wheel moves to the human while a `hook.dialog` is already pending (a human editing key or `viola pause`), the wrapper answers that pending request with `null` at once and clears its `dialog_id`, so a dialog held by the hook is never hidden from the human for the rest of the deadline. `budget-paused` gates only `send`, never `hook.dialog` or `answer`, because a running turn finishes. Rationale: `viola run` owns the real stdin, so it knows byte provenance exactly. The prototype paused twice on harness turns before the classifier was fixed.
-- **[Links] A link `driver → driven` is recorded when a `send` or `answer` is issued from inside a wrapped session to another instance, or by an explicit `viola link <driver> <driven>`. `viola unlink` removes it.** A call counts as coming from inside a wrapped session when the calling process has `VIOLA_NAME` set. The CLI and the MCP server pass that name as `from` in the request `params`, and the driven instance's wrapper appends a `link` event to its `events.ndjson` only when that `driver → driven` pair is not already in its link set. Repeated sends and answers add no line, and an explicit `viola link` of an existing pair adds none and returns `{}`, so `since` is the time of the first link after any `unlink`. `unlink` appends an `unlink` event. Links are events in the log. The link set in the snapshot, and so in `/api/links`, is derived by replaying them. Rationale: D5 asks the GUI to show which sessions are linked, and the log stays the single source of truth for that as it is for everything else. As landed (chunk 2026-10-10-session-links) only the implicit link is built. The driven wrapper's `LinkSet` (`src/run/links.rs`) records it on a `send` after the second wheel and running-turn reads and directly before `send-issued`, and on an `answer` once every refusal has passed and the response's kind matched the pending dialog, before the answer is taken. Five shapes of it stand on the operator's word and are not ruled by the founder: a refused `send` or `answer` records no link; a `from` equal to the wrapper's own name links nothing; the `link` line's `source` is `wrapper`; the record sits directly before `send-issued`, so a send that is issued and then reads `not-delivered` has linked; and a `link` line with no string `ts` reads `since: "unknown"`. A link that cannot be recorded is `-32603`, with nothing issued or the answer not taken. The set is restored from the log at every start. The explicit `viola link` / `viola unlink` and their two channel methods are not built: the wrapper answers `link` and `unlink` `-32601`, no subcommand exists, and no product path appends an `unlink` event (`LinkSet::remove` is unit-tested and has no caller). They are held: the boundary widening they carry is not ratified (the wrapper serving two more methods, the two verbs writing their frame after the liveness-only pre-check, and their snapshot read without the strict-modes check), and the founder's own answer of 2026-10-13, relayed verbatim by the Viola overseer, is that no explicit verb is built before Epoch 6, the three crossings staying unbuilt and unratified. The Epoch 6 working-route entry "Explicit session links", after "Windows endpoint admission" and "Server verification before any frame", carries the hold (moved there from the end of Epoch 5 by that answer; first moved from "The board: viola list" by the review card the founder approved, chunk 2026-10-11-the-board-viola-list).
-- **[Budget Governor] Soft stop at the turn boundary.** Once a configured threshold is crossed (defaults `five_hour` ≥ 90 %, `seven_day` ≥ 85 %), automated `send` is refused with `budget-paused` through the same refusal path as `human-typing`. Human keys are never blocked, and a running turn finishes. The pause lifts when `resets_at` passes or on `viola release --budget`. `release --budget` leaves the wheel where it is, and a plain `release` returns the wheel to `driver` without lifting a budget pause. `release --budget` is sent to one instance's wrapper and lifts the pause for that instance only, until the crossed window's `resets_at` passes, so the next over-threshold reading does not re-pause it. The wrapper evaluates its gate from `budget.json`, read under `budget.json.lock`, at three points: at start, when a `send` arrives, and at each `turn-ended`. It never polls. A crossed threshold, a passed `resets_at` or an expired override takes effect at the next of these points, and only then is `budget-gate` appended. So a snapshot can still read `budget_paused: true` after the reset, until a `send` arrives or a turn ends, and every surface that shows the snapshot inherits that. The wrapper mirrors its budget-pause state, including any override, to the instance snapshot, where `list` and `ui` read it. As landed (chunk 2026-10-10-budget-governor; `src/run/budget.rs`, one `BudgetGate` per wrapper, no thread and no timer): a window is crossed only when its `used_percentage` is known and at or above its threshold and its `resets_at` is known, parses as RFC 3339 and is later than now, so a window whose `resets_at` is `"unknown"`, unparseable or passed never pauses. With both windows crossed the refusal names `five-hour`. The `send` point sits after the text refusals and the first `human-typing` read and before the running-turn read, so the order is `control-character` → `empty-text` → `human-typing` → `budget-paused` → `turn-running` → the readiness gate's wait; the gate is read once per `send`, never again after that wait. `answer`, `wait`, `last`, `pause` and `hook.dialog` do not read the gate (read in the diff: its five product call sites; since chunk 2026-10-10-session-links the root case `path6_a_closed_gate_leaves_answer_wait_last_and_pause_alone`, `tests/cli_budget.rs`, drives `answer`, `wait`, `last` and `pause` under one closed gate, each giving the exit code and the document it gives under an open one; no case drives `hook.dialog` under a closed gate). An override covers both windows until its end and is dropped at the next point after that end. It does not outlive its wrapper: every start, a revived one included, evaluates from `budget.json` alone and restores none. `release --budget` with no pause standing records nothing. `budget-gate` is appended, and the snapshot's `budget_paused` and `budget_override_until` are rewritten, only when the triple (paused, window, override end) changes, both before the evaluation returns; a record that cannot land is an error and the recorded state stays as it was. The thresholds are read once, at the wrapper's start, and no value of either switches the gate off. The gate is fail-open: no reading, an `"unknown"` figure, a passed or unparseable `resets_at`, an unreadable or refused `budget.json` and a bad threshold each leave it open or at its default. The decision takes the wall-clock instant as an argument (no clock seam). With figures missing, viola uses the newest reading from any wrapped session and shows its age. With no reading at all it records `unknown` and does not block. The statusline is wrapped only for sessions started by `viola run`, through a per-session settings override that then runs the user's own statusline command unchanged. As landed (chunk 2026-10-10-statusline-pass-through) the wrap is Unix only: on Windows no override is written and no command is run, and that half is owed to the working-route entry "Windows-only live measurements" (the founder's word on the cut, live, 2026-10-10, relayed verbatim by the operator). A reading is recorded only when the payload holds `rate_limits`. Rationale: the per-model weekly window is hidden (claude-code#91920), so thresholds are set low. The user's global statusline config is never touched, and a visible, conservative governor supports the "ordinary individual usage" posture.
